  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalled frontal boundary will produce showers and\na few thunderstorms over southern Virginia and northeast North \nCarolina today. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible.\n\nAs of 1015Z, a frontal boundary is stalled across far southern \nVA with some isolated showers ongoing over SE VA and NE NC. A\nswath of 1-2\" of rain occurred between 07z-9z from southern\nSouthampton County through southern Chesapeake and VA Beach.\nThis activity has since dissipated. The aforementioned boundary\nwill remain fairly stationary through the day today, before \nlifting back north as a warm from by this evening and \ntonight/early Tues AM. This feature will be the focus for\nadditional showers and isolated strong tstm development again \ntoday, primarily this afternoon/evening, There is a low end SVR \nthreat today, mainly for isold damaging wind gusts over NE NC, \nand also a SLGT ERO for all of NE NC and a marginal ERO across \nsouthern VA for a few instances of flash flooding due to heavy \nrainfall potential. Dew points will be highest across southern \nareas today (mid-70s) with PWATs in excess of 2\", hence the \nMRGL/SLGT ERO. Highs today will be in the mid 80s with the some \nlow 80s along the immediate coast. As the warm front lifts north\ntonight, we'll see scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms\nand locally heavy rainfall will be possible. Lows tonight in \nthe upr 60s to mid 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Periods of unsettled weather are expected\nthrough Wednesday. Temperatures will trend below normal by \nThursday and Friday.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday, the severe threat looks to be \nincreasing as a much stronger trough will start to move out of \nthe Midwest and into the Mid-Atlantic. Tuesday looks to be the \nbest SVR day as shear looks to be the strongest and multiple \nshortwaves will move through which could initiate storms. Some \nof the wind profiles based of model soundings show the potential\nfor discrete storms ahead of a possible line of storms coming \nout of the NW. At the surface, a strong cold front will move \ninto place Wednesday helping to initiate thunderstorms \npotentially producing another round of severe weather. There \nremains slight uncertainty but models have hinted on the \npotential for a recovering atmosphere ahead of the front that is\nfavorable for severe weather. The primary hazard at this time \nlooks to be strong to severe wind gusts but other hazards could \npotentially evolve. For both Tuesday and Wednesday, most of the\narea will be under a slight ERO. With PW's remaining high and\nshowers and thunderstorms continuing, the flood risk could\nincrease, especially for areas that receive multiple rounds of\nheavy rain. The QPF forecast at this time calls for 2-4\" of rain\nbasically from RIC on south and east through Wed night. Once \nthis cold front moves through, slightly cooler (below normal) \ntemperatures are favored for Thursday/Friday. However, the risk \nof heavy rain due to additional showers and thunderstorms looks \nto continue across southeast VA and northeast NC through the end\nof the week.",
